[2026-08-28] --- [18:33:05]
Software as a Service (SaaS) is basically the model of renting software over the internet instead of installing it on your local hard drive. Three common examples are Google Workspace, Spotify and Salesforce.

Benefits:
Convenience and accessibility, Since you can log in from any device on the planet and pick up right where you left off, not having to deal with server maintenance, security updates and storage.

Drawbacks:
- You never actually own the software
- You're locked into endless subscription fees
- If your internet drops or their servers crash, you are completely locked out of your own tools and files

When it comes to the rumors about the next version of Windows becoming a SaaS product, I honestly don't really care. I'm a power user of open-source software, specifically Linux and I strongly advocate for local, offline software and operating systems. My main reason for avoiding the cloud is how quickly it can ruin your workflow. A while back, I used AutoDesk's Fusion 360 for 3D modeling, and it was a frustrating experience. It would constantly lose its connection to the server right in the middle of a project. Even worse, it would sometimes force mandatory updates first thing in the morning, completely rendering my productivity to zero while I just sat there waiting for it to finish. Because of that, I strictly prefer offline software for individual use so I can dictate when updates happen.

SaaS definitely has its place in the corporate world. Enterprises with nationwide workforce that heavily relies on real-time contributions, jumping to a SaaS platform is absolutely worth the investment. But for a single user trying to get personal work done, local software will always win.

[2026-08-28] --- [18:39:00]
I completely agree about subscription fatigue—it is ridiculous to think about essentially renting the ability to use your own physical computer hardware. Like you mentioned, if your Wi-Fi drops, a purely cloud-based Windows OS would turn a perfectly good machine into a useless brick. For everyday personal computing, forcing a subscription model onto the OS just feels like a cash grab that compromises basic reliability.

[2026-08-28] --- [18:42:02]
I completely agree about subscription fatigue—it is ridiculous to think about essentially renting the ability to use your own physical computer hardware. Like you mentioned, if your Wi-Fi drops, a purely cloud-based Windows OS would turn a perfectly good machine into a useless brick. Not even mentioning the total lack of control over your own system, you can't dictate when updates should happen for example. For everyday personal computing, forcing a subscription model onto the OS just feels like a cash grab that compromises basic reliability.

[2026-08-28] --- [18:44:35]
The idea that automatic updates are a benefit is definitely a double-edged sword. While not having to manually patch bugs is nice, forced updates can completely kill your productivity. I used to deal with this using AutoDesk's Fusion 360—it would run mandatory updates first thing in the morning and I'd just be sitting there unable to get any work done. That total loss of control is exactly why I have decided to give a 'lesser' fully offline software a try, in order to replace the subscription hungry model.

Having to pay a monthly subscription just to boot up and use a computer you already bought is ridiculous. If Windows ever actually goes full SaaS like you mentioned, a lot of people are going to be forced to hunt down free, open-source alternatives just to keep their hardware usable without paying a continuous toll. Local software gives you actual ownership of your machine.

