[2026-08-29] --- [18:43:33]
Less is definitely more when it comes to slideshows. The point of a presentation is to keep the audience engaged while not forcing a reading assignment to them. When a slide has just a few keywords or a single strong visual, it forces people to actually look at you and listen to what you're saying to get the full story.

If you just cram a slide full of text and read it word-for-word (verbatim) you lose the room instantly. Been there, done that and also received that.. When a dense wall of text is on screen, people will tend to read faster than you can speak, they finish reading the slide in ten seconds and go on messing with their phones and doing everything else, other than listening, while just nodding. So the point is to not read directly from the screen, but to present less text, but some useful data that forces you into explaining the thing.

[2026-08-30] --- [14:07:57]
Her slideshow shouldn't have paragraphs explaining historical fashion, sewing techniques, or fabric types. Instead, the content should be almost entirely visual. Think of high-resolution photos of fabric textures, comparisons of sketches versus the final product (stage outfits) or even short video clips showing how a specific garment moves/presents on certain lighting conditions.

Again "Less is More". If Julie makes the mistake of inserting a wall of text and simply reading off of it, she's going to lose the room pretty fast. By keeping text to an absolute minimum (maybe just a character's name or a historical event) on the slide, she forces the audience to focus on the images while actually listening to her explaining the context.

Again, the slides should just be the visual proof, she needs to be the one actually delivering the message.

