[2026-09-01] --- [13:45:26]
Discussion 6:
What is the value of using Excel to answer what-if questions about data?

[2026-09-01] --- [13:46:43]
Business activities:

Order fulfillment and deliveries: You can use large worksheets to track hundreds of daily delivery routes and run What-If analyses to see exactly how route changes impact fuel costs and drop-off times.

Inventory management: Using basic formulas to track warehouse stock levels, then throwing that data into a pie chart to easily see which products are eating up the most shelf space.

Non-business activities:

Tracking functional strength training: You can log your reps, sets, and weights for kettlebell and sandbag routines, then use line charts to visually track your performance trends and strength progression over the year.

Personal investment monitoring: Managing data across checking accounts and crypto wallets by using formulas to calculate your total net worth, and charting your asset distribution to see exactly where your money is sitting.

[2026-09-01] --- [13:49:15]
I believe that the main value of Excel's what-if analysis is being able to simulate different scenarios without messing up your actual data or making expensive real-world mistakes. It basically lets you look into the future based on changing variables.

For example, if you are managing order fulfillment and deliveries (I have worked in this field before) you can run a what-if scenario to see exactly how a 15% spike in fuel costs or adding two extra routes will impact your weekly budget.

On a personal level, you could use it for investment monitoring to see how a sudden drop in prices would affect your overall net worth. Instead of guessing and hoping for the best, you just plug in the hypothetical numbers and let the software instantly spit out the best and worst-case scenarios. The result? No more emotion and guesswork in the decision making.

[2026-09-01] --- [13:51:56]
Business activities:

Order fulfillment and deliveries: You can use large worksheets to track hundreds of daily delivery routes and use basic formulas to calculate total weekly mileage. Charting the data helps visually pinpoint peak delivery times so you can schedule better.

Inventory management: Tracking warehouse stock levels using formulas, and throwing that data into a pie chart to easily see which products are eating up the most shelf space at a quick glance.

Non-business activities:

* Tracking functional strength training: You can log your reps, sets, and weights for kettlebell and sandbag routines, then use line charts to visually track your performance trends and strength progression over the year.
* Personal investment monitoring: Managing data across checking accounts and crypto wallets by using formulas to calculate your total balances, and charting your asset distribution to see exactly where your money is actually sitting.

[2026-09-01] --- [13:52:56]
