[2026-09-10] --- [14:45:16]
That example shows perfectly why visual aids matter way more than text. Instead of throwing up heavy paragraphs explaining gravity or planetary orbits, the class will check out instantly. Visuals like diagrams act as an anchor to keep people focused without overwhelming them with raw data.

It makes all the sense, since everyone reads way faster than a person can speak. If the audience finishes reading your slide in five seconds, they will just pull out their phones and stop listening to you.

[2026-09-10] --- [14:49:18]
The idea of highlighting practical details like hidden Velcro seams or reinforced stitching is interesting, but it only works if it stays purely visual. If Julie just writes "use Velcro for quick changes" as a bullet point, it instantly becomes a boring lecture. A high-res close-up photo of that hidden seam does all the heavy lifting.

This perfectly aligns with the "less is more" rule for text. By just throwing up a side-by-side photo of the fabric under normal light versus stage lighting without any paragraphs explaining it, she avoids making herself useless.

[2026-09-10] --- [14:57:20]
Showing before-and-after pictures and raw fabric sketches without heavy text is the best way to handle this. No one likes to read through dense text when they're supposed to receive the info spoken by the presenter. By relying strictly on those visual examples, she forces the room to look at the screen for context but actually listen to her explain the actual design process. It is all about turning yourself valueable.

